Let's see, how does picking up work in vanilla NetHack 3.4.3:

(If WIZARD is defined then a debug capable version of NetHack is 
compiled. If such version is compiled and the game is started in debug 
mode then the variable wizard is true.)

dopickup() in hack.c is the function that is executed when ',' is 
pressed. It checks if you are able to pick up anything but doesn't do 
the actual movement of items from a floor location to the heros 
inventory. In the end it calls the function pickup().

pickup() in pickup.c is the function that handles the picking up no 
matter if it's commanded or autopickup. This function doesn't move the 
items from the floor location to heros inventory but calls the function 
pickup_object().

pickup_object() in pickup.c is the function that handles evading 
artifacts, touch petrification, rider revivivals, picking up coins, BUC 
change and crumbling of scroll of scare monster. It also informs if you 
are not able to lift what you try to pick up. When all these checks are 
made a call to function pick_obj() is done.

pick_obj() in pickup.c is the function that removes the object from its 
present location. It also puts it on your shopbill if it was removed 
from a costly spot in a shop. It then calls addinv().

addinv() in invent.c  does the following:

Panics if the object is not free.

Calls function addinv_core1() which notes if you've picked up any of the 
invocation artifacts, the Amulet or your own quest artifact.

Exits if it was gold you picked up as pickup_object() handled that already.

Calls merged() to merge the object into the inventory if possible. The 
call of merged() is not depending on definition of WIZARD or the value 
of wizard.

If merged() was unsuccesful the object is inserted into the inventory, a 
suitable inventory letter is assigned to it and object is marked to be 
in the inventory. Inventory is also reordered in case of fixed inventory 
letters.

Calls addinv_core2() which handles the possible luck effects of the 
picked up object.

Calls carry_obj_effects() which handles other than luck effects that 
carrying the object might have.

Calls update_inventory() which updates the floating inventory display in 
windowed environments.

Exits.

merged() merges two objects together if possible. This functions working 
is not depending on the definition of WIZARD or the value of wizaed.


So in the end the picking up process in vanilla NetHack 3.4.3 is not 
depending on the setting of WIZARD. So even if non-debug version is 
compiled the code is the same. In the whole invent.c the only thing that 
is affected by WIZARD is that (debuging)wizards are capable on wishing 
venom and thus can leave it to others in bones file. So if a debugging 
version of the game is compiled then any character can have venom in 
their inventory.

So the argument that picking up in a normal game differs from picking up 
in debugging game is bogus.

